WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.130
Film Courage: I know you’re a fan of Joseph
Campbell (The Hero’s Journey).

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:03.130
Adam Skelter, Author/Story Artist/Screenwriter:
Yes.

00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:06.319
Film Courage: And most people are.

00:00:06.319 --> 00:00:10.370
There’s not really any criticism of him…is
there?

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:15.460
Adam: Well, I’ve learned so much from Joseph
Campbell.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:16.970
I discovered him pretty young.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:22.160
I was 16 or 17 when I started reading him
and I was amazed by it and of course the thing

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:28.020
that was nice about it was that he was the
foray into [Carl] Jung and the theory of archetypes

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:32.180
and things like that.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:37.141
While Campbell is dense, it’s a totally
different thing to delve into, Jung and [Sigmund]

00:00:37.141 --> 00:00:40.050
Freud and all those things.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:46.819
And part of the issue is…and I know the
hero and the hero’s journey and people regard

00:00:46.819 --> 00:00:51.329
the hero one of the central archetypes of
the character.

00:00:51.329 --> 00:01:03.379
But I think Joseph Campbell illuminated certain
insights into story that were incredibly helpful

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:07.149
but I don’t think it’s enough of a complete
theory and I plan on doing a video [On Adam’s

00:01:07.149 --> 00:01:09.880
Youtube channel] later just to get more specific.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:16.390
But just to give a teaser ultimately when
we look at a hero, hero is a very specific

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:17.390
kind of character.

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:20.310
That’s why I tend to refer to things as
a protagonist versus a hero.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:30.070
A hero is someone that exhibits certain virtues
and values that are considered ideal and usually

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:34.250
the story is about them testing their medal.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:41.560
But the story can be so much more than just
kind of a vulgar propaganda on exhibiting

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.950
ideal virtues.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:48.450
And that’s why I think the hero’s journey
is brilliant and fantastic.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:54.270
One thing that I liked about Joseph Campbell
is that he was exploring the universality

00:01:54.270 --> 00:02:02.560
of story structure but I don’t think it’s
enough of a complete paradigm because when

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:12.080
we look at why we tell stories, it doesn’t
quite account for the initiation of the tribe

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:21.190
or into the tribe and that’s what I think
is very fascinating to me is my next video

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:22.320
is about tribalism actually.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.260
I’m in the process of talking about tribalism.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:28.360
I set up the relationship between religion
and story in one video and I’m going to

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:30.400
tie into tribalism.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:34.410
And I’m defining tribalism in a very specific
way.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:44.550
I’m going to go into the biological imperatives
that form tribalism (in this little teaser).

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:51.870
There are different groups of people who define
themselves according to what their objectives

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:52.870
are.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:58.350
So for example a community is a group of people
with a common interest.

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:01.580
A movement is a group of people with a common
cause.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:05.360
A tribe is a group of people with a common
enemy.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:09.960
They have very, very different ways of functioning.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:19.290
If you have an enemy that usually creates
a…loyalty becomes very important, it’s

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.120
a virtue within the tribe.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:28.390
Within a community of broad, open interests
you don’t necessarily need loyalty in order

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:29.520
to function.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.890
You can just have voluntary interactions.

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:42.600
With tribalism because it defines itself by
its enemy and it presents it as a threat and

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.790
therefore hierarchy becomes very important.

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:56.080
A lot of stories throughout most of the history
of humanity were designed to help create those

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:57.960
hierarchies.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:05.390
So because most tribes were surviving in the
context of a lot of constant threat (perpetual

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:14.040
threat) which means they had to live in very
rigid communities or rigid groups of people.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:19.220
So the narratives in the stories they would
tell were always about hero structure.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:27.000
They were designed to help people incorporate
these values and these virtues as vital to

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.510
survival.

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:37.680
So because of that, this hero’s journey
was ultimately a kind of propaganda because

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:43.590
it was designed to drive people’s behavior
and value system to be subject and loyal to

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:44.940
the tribe.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:50.350
That’s why I think stories that are more
engaged and more engaged in moral ambiguity

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:55.560
have much more interesting things to tell
because they are not just interested in exposing

00:04:55.560 --> 00:05:02.530
and propagating specific narrow values that
emphasize loyalty but instead will actually

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:06.010
explore the nature of the human condition.

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:07.690
And that’s what I think is a much more interesting
thing.

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:14.870
If we can understand the way that story is
a kind of exploration of values rather than

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:21.560
just a propagation of values, then I think
we actually have more meaningful discourse

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:25.090
in understanding story and humanity.

